<header><a class="brand" href="/"><img src="/hanji-mark.svg" alt=""/>Hanji</a><a href="/projects/document/#get-started">Request access ↗</a></header>
<main>
 <section class="intro"><p class="eyebrow">GETTING STARTED · DESKTOP PREVIEW</p><h1>A little room<br/>for your next idea.</h1><p class="lede">From installing Hanji to a workspace that feels yours.<br class="desktop"/> A few choices, then you’re ready to begin.</p>
 <div class="preview-access"><div><span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span><strong>Available by invitation</strong><p>The Mac preview is shared directly. Public downloads aren’t available yet.</p></div><a class="primary" href="/projects/document/#get-started">Request the Mac preview <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
 </section>
 <nav class="journey" aria-label="In this guide"><a href="#install"><b>01</b> Install Hanji</a><a href="#workspace"><b>02</b> Make your space</a><a href="#begin"><b>03</b> Begin with one thing</a></nav>
 <section class="chapter" id="install"><div class="chapter-title"><span>01</span><h2>One app. A calm start.</h2></div><div class="chapter-body"><p>Already have the preview? Follow the instructions included with your copy.</p><ol class="steps"><li><b>Open your download</b><p>Unzip the preview archive. You’ll find Hanji.app and a “Start here” guide. Use the build shared for your Mac.</p></li><li><b>Move Hanji to Applications</b><p>Drag Hanji.app into the Applications folder, then open it from there. This keeps your app in a familiar place for future updates.</p></li><li><b>Create your first workspace</b><p>Choose <strong>Create your workspace</strong> on the welcome screen. Already have a workspace folder or a team address? Those doors are there too.</p></li></ol><details><summary>If macOS can’t verify the preview</summary><p>Signing and notarization depend on the build you received. Confirm the source with the person who shared it before opening. <a href="https://support.apple.com/102445" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Apple’s guide to opening apps safely ↗</a></p></details></div></section>
 <section class="chapter" id="workspace"><div class="chapter-title"><span>02</span><h2>Make it yours.</h2></div><div class="chapter-body"><div class="workspace-demo" aria-label="Example workspace: Field notes, with Think, Plan and Document"><div class="demo-top"><span class="monogram">F</span><span><b>Field notes</b><small>Your workspace</small></span><span class="demo-check" aria-hidden="true">✓</span></div><div class="demo-products"><span>Think</span><span>Plan</span><span>Document</span></div><div class="demo-bottom"><span>Starting point</span><b>Fresh workspace</b></div></div><figure class="guide-capture"><img src="/assets/current/preview-onboarding.webp" width="1280" height="900" alt="The actual Hanji workspace review, with a fictional workspace" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/><figcaption>Current desktop setup · fictional workspace · English interface</figcaption></figure><ol class="steps"><li><b>A name and a few useful tools</b><p>Choose Think for visual ideas, Plan for next steps, and Document for knowledge. Turn on what you need; you can change this later.</p></li><li><b>Start fresh, or bring your content</b><p>Start fresh lets Hanji prepare local folders for you. If you already have Markdown, a repository, a tracker or canvases, choose Bring existing content instead.</p></li><li><b>Secure it, then take a look</b><p>Choose an owner password, review your choices, and create the workspace. The desktop app signs you in on this Mac.</p></li></ol><p class="note">Your workspace files stay on your Mac. An AI subscription isn’t required. You can connect an agent later.</p></div></section>
 <section class="chapter" id="begin"><div class="chapter-title"><span>03</span><h2>Begin with one thing.</h2></div><div class="chapter-body"><div class="first-actions"><article><span>Think</span><h3>A blank canvas.<br/>A first idea.</h3><p>Create a canvas and add a sticky. Give the idea room to grow.</p></article><article><span>Plan</span><h3>One next step<br/>you can act on.</h3><p>Create an issue. A title is enough to get the thought out of your head.</p></article><article><span>Document</span><h3>Keep something<br/>worth returning to.</h3><p>Open your welcome page, edit a line, and make the space your own.</p></article></div><p class="note">You don’t need to configure everything on day one. Add folders, people and agents in Settings as your workspace grows.</p></div></section>
 <section class="help"><h2>A hand when you need it.</h2><div><a href="https://hanji.ink/docs/">Read the handbook ↗</a><a href="/projects/document/#get-started">Request preview access ↗</a></div><details><summary>Prefer to run from source?</summary><p>Source access is shared with preview participants. If you have a checkout, follow its README for the current Node, pnpm and Git setup.</p></details></section>
</main><footer><a href="/">Hanji</a><span>Think freely. Keep what matters.</span></footer>
</body></html>
